The '''Maros Grand Mosque''' is a significant architectural landmark located within the [[Crescent Belt]] of Northeast [[Fosia]]. Constructed around the 16th century, the mosque is a primary example of Marosan design, characterized by hybrid artistic traditions that blend local elements with the maritime culture of the Bayani people. Its establishment coincided with the rise of the [[Sultanate of Maros]], a period defined by the growth of trade-centered port cities and the consolidation of Islamic institutions across the archipelago.

The mosque serves as a testament to the gradual and peaceful spread of Islam in the region, which occurred primarily through merchant networks and intermarriage rather than military conquest. As a focal point of the Ḥizām al-Hilāl, the structure reflects a history of cosmopolitan exchange driven by monsoon-driven sea lanes. Today, it remains an essential site for religious life and scholarly tradition, representing the shared heritage of the coastal and archipelagic territories from [[Nusaraya]] to [[Pangkajene]].

